@page "/learn/wasm-hosted-services"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Background services in the browser · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Background services in the browser</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your bell checks for order updates. Does it keep checking when you change pages?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · WebAssembly · Interactive WebAssembly · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> in the browser, services you register with <code>AddHostedService</code> now
        start on their own, just like on a server. In .NET 10 you could write the
        <code>AddHostedService</code> call, but the service never ran, and nothing told you. You had to start it
        yourself.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is an <i>Orders</i> page with a bell at the top. The bell runs in the browser and asks the server for
        order updates every 3 seconds. It shows how many times it has checked, since when, and how many updates it
        found. The <i>Customers</i> page has no bell.
    </p>
    <p>
        This first bell runs its own timer. When you leave the page, the timer goes with it. That's normal for a
        component, and it's why you'd move the checking into a service. But in .NET 10, a service registered with
        <code>AddHostedService</code> never ran. Fig. 1 shows both. At the top is the live bell. Below it is a table
        recorded from a .NET 10 app, counting how often each version checked.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="A bell that polls by itself" Url="demo/hosted-services/orders/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: the bell polls by itself" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Wait about ten seconds until the bell says <b>checked the server 3×</b>. Click <b>Customers</b>, wait, then
            click <b>Orders</b>: the bell starts counting again from zero, with a new <b>since</b> time. Now look at
            the recorded table. Its <b>AddHostedService</b> row stayed at <b>0</b> the whole time, with no error. That's
            the line Fig. 2 uses.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The page owns the timer</h2>
    <p>The timer lives in the bell, and the bell lives in the page:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What lives how long in the browser</span>
        <pre class="snip">Browser tab
└─ WebAssembly runtime            <span class="ok">✓ keeps running as you change pages</span>
   ├─ Orders page
   │  └─ PollingBell
   │     └─ PeriodicTimer         <span class="err">disposed when you leave Orders</span>
   └─ services                    <span class="ok">✓ live as long as the runtime</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        Leave Orders and the bell is thrown away, timer and count included. Come back and a new bell starts from zero.
        The checking should live in a service instead. But in .NET 10, nothing started that service for you.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Make it a hosted service</h2>
    <p>
        Move the checking into a <code>BackgroundService</code>. Register it once, and pass that same instance to
        <code>AddHostedService</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip">public sealed class OrderWatcher(HttpClient http) : <span class="hl">BackgroundService</span> { ... }

<span class="dim">// Program.cs, in the WebAssembly project</span>
builder.Services.AddSingleton&lt;OrderWatcher&gt;();
<span class="hl">builder.Services.AddHostedService(sp =&gt; sp.GetRequiredService&lt;OrderWatcher&gt;());</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The bell no longer checks anything. It just listens to the watcher. <code>AddSingleton</code> gives every bell
        the same watcher. The host is the part of .NET that starts your app and its services, and the lambda tells it
        to start that same watcher. If you wrote <code>AddHostedService&lt;OrderWatcher&gt;()</code> instead, you'd
        get two watchers: the host would run one, and the bells would listen to the other, which never checks.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same pages, with a hosted watcher" Url="demo/hosted-services/orders"
                Label=".NET 11: AddHostedService">
        <Caption>
            Wait until the bell says <b>checked the server 3×</b>. Click <b>Customers</b>, wait, then click
            <b>Orders</b>. The <b>since</b> time is the same and the count kept going while you were away.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the code:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Client/Program.cs" Feature="wasm-hosted-services">
        <Caption>The registration: one watcher, started by the host.</Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Client/HostedServices/OrderWatcher.cs" Feature="wasm-hosted-services">
        <Caption>
            A normal <code>BackgroundService</code>, the same kind you'd use on a server. It checks every 3 seconds
            until the tab closes.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Client/HostedServices/OrderBell.razor" Feature="wasm-hosted-services">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's bell. It only listens, so it can come and go without stopping the checks. Nothing here is new;
            the highlights just show what replaced the timer.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="6" Path="Client/HostedServices/PollingBell.razor" Feature="wasm-hosted-services">
        <Caption>
            For comparison: Fig. 1's bell. Its timer starts with the component, and <code>Dispose</code> stops it.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Check from the page, or start it yourself</h2>
    <p>
        The table in Fig. 1 came from a .NET 10 app that tried three ways. Here's the code behind each row. The numbers
        are how many times each one had ticked (once a second) after 5 seconds on Orders, on Customers, and back on
        Orders.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three watchers in one .NET 10 app, and their ticks</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">PeriodicTimer in the component</pre>
                <span class="why">5, then gone on Customers, then back to 1</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ starts again from nothing</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">builder.Services
  .AddHostedService(...);</pre>
                <span class="why">won't compile without the Hosting.Abstractions package; with it, stays at 0</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ never starts, with no warning</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">var host = builder.Build();
_ = watcher.StartAsync(
    CancellationToken.None);
await host.RunAsync();</pre>
                <span class="why">the "Started by hand" row: 5, 8, 10</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ keeps counting across pages</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Starting it yourself worked, but only for your own services. A hosted service added by a library, like
        OpenTelemetry's, never started.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>The host starts them</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the WebAssembly host calls <code>StartAsync</code> on every hosted service, and waits for it, before
        it renders anything. The hosting types come with the WebAssembly SDK, so there's no package to add.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>It starts before the first render.</b> So keep <code>StartAsync</code> quick. A <code>BackgroundService</code> is fine: its <code>StartAsync</code> hands back control as soon as your <code>ExecuteAsync</code> starts waiting on something.</li>
        <li><b>One thread.</b> In the browser, .NET runs on a single thread. Waiting, like this check, is fine. A long calculation freezes the page.</li>
        <li><b>One copy per tab.</b> It starts with the first WebAssembly component on the page. A reload or a new tab starts a fresh copy. Closing the tab ends it without calling <code>StopAsync</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Sometimes a component survives a page change.</b> When you follow a link, Blazor usually swaps in the new page without a full reload. If the new page has the same WebAssembly component in the same place, Blazor keeps it instead of throwing it away. So if you put Fig. 1's bell on every page, its timer would keep going. It would still stop on any page without the bell.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
